# 0606 — SERIES 600 WIDGETS

## LEGAiSEE COMMANDCENTER KNOWLEDGE SYSTEM

Version: 1.0
Status: ACTIVE GOVERNANCE STANDARD

---

# PURPOSE

The Widgets subsystem defines the modular building blocks of the LEGAiSEE CommandCenter User Interface.

Widgets are self-contained interface components that present information, accept user interaction, or provide operational controls.

Every screen within CommandCenter is composed of one or more widgets.

Widgets provide a standardized, reusable method for constructing interfaces while maintaining consistency, scalability, and governance.

---

# CORE PRINCIPLE

Interfaces should be assembled rather than hardcoded.

Every reusable interface function should exist as a widget.

Widgets are independent components that can be combined to create dashboards, workspaces, reports, investigative views, and operational consoles.

---

# OBJECTIVES

The Widgets subsystem shall:

* promote interface consistency
* reduce duplicated development
* simplify maintenance
* support modular expansion
* standardize operator interaction
* encourage reusable design
* maintain governance compliance

---

# WIDGET ARCHITECTURE

Each widget consists of four logical layers:

```text
Presentation
      │
      ▼
Interaction
      │
      ▼
Data Binding
      │
      ▼
Business Logic
```

Presentation defines appearance.

Interaction defines user behavior.

Data Binding connects the widget to CommandCenter data.

Business Logic determines widget functionality.

---

# WIDGET CHARACTERISTICS

Every widget should be:

* modular
* reusable
* self-contained
* configurable
* responsive
* accessible
* testable
* governable

Widgets should have clearly defined responsibilities.

---

# WIDGET CATEGORIES

CommandCenter widgets include, but are not limited to:

---

## Information Widgets

Present operational information.

Examples:

* System Status
* Project Summary
* Investigation Overview
* KPI Cards
* Notifications

---

## Intelligence Widgets

Present intelligence generated by the system.

Examples:

* Relationship Summary
* Graph Insights
* Recommendations
* Predictions
* Timeline Highlights

---

## Navigation Widgets

Provide movement throughout the system.

Examples:

* Sidebar Navigation
* Breadcrumbs
* Quick Launch
* Favorites
* Recent Items

---

## Operational Widgets

Support active work.

Examples:

* Task Queue
* Active Excavations
* Running Jobs
* Command Queue
* Scheduler Status

---

## Investigation Widgets

Assist analytical workflows.

Examples:

* Evidence Viewer
* Artifact Browser
* Entity Inspector
* Timeline Panel
* Relationship Explorer

---

## Administrative Widgets

Provide system administration functions.

Examples:

* User Activity
* System Health
* Storage Usage
* Security Status
* Audit Summary

---

# WIDGET LIFECYCLE

A widget follows a standard lifecycle.

```text
Initialize
      │
      ▼
Load Configuration
      │
      ▼
Bind Data
      │
      ▼
Render
      │
      ▼
Accept Interaction
      │
      ▼
Refresh
      │
      ▼
Dispose
```

Widgets should release resources when no longer active.

---

# CONFIGURATION

Widgets should support configuration including:

* title
* size
* placement
* refresh interval
* permissions
* data source
* filters
* display options

Configuration should not require modification of widget code.

---

# DATA SOURCES

Widgets may receive information from:

* Kernel
* Modules
* Engines
* Projects
* Cases
* Clients
* Graph Database
* Memory Store
* File Repository
* External APIs

Data access remains governed by system permissions.

---

# INTERACTION

Widgets may support:

* click
* selection
* filtering
* searching
* sorting
* expansion
* collapsing
* drag-and-drop
* contextual menus

Interaction behavior should remain consistent across the entire interface.

---

# PERFORMANCE

Widgets shall:

* load independently
* update efficiently
* minimize unnecessary processing
* cache appropriate data
* fail gracefully

One malfunctioning widget should not compromise the surrounding interface.

---

# GOVERNANCE

Widgets shall never:

* bypass permissions
* expose unauthorized information
* execute privileged operations directly
* alter system state without validation
* conceal operational errors

All widget actions remain subject to CommandCenter governance.

---

# RELATIONSHIP TO OTHER SERIES

### Series 300

Provides excavation and intelligence data displayed by widgets.

### Series 400

Provides graph intelligence, reasoning, prediction, and memory.

### Series 500

Provides interaction models and operational workflows.

### Series 600

Uses widgets as the fundamental construction units for every interface.

---

# SYSTEM ROLE

The Widgets subsystem functions as the modular interface framework of the LEGAiSEE CommandCenter.

It enables the system to build flexible, reusable, and governed user interfaces by combining standardized components that present information and facilitate interaction.

Widgets are the building blocks from which every dashboard, workspace, and operational console is constructed.

---

# GUIDING PRINCIPLE

> **Pages are composed of workspaces.
> Workspaces are composed of panels.
> Panels are composed of widgets.
> Widgets are the reusable building blocks of the CommandCenter experience.**

